; adversarial test: forall-intro must reject a conclusion that mentions a fresh
; constant introduced *inside* the `let` block but never generalized over -- `helper`
; here is not one of `let`'s own bound variables, it's a bystander declared inside the
; block; if it were allowed to leak into the generalized conclusion, `forall x` would
; be quantifying over the wrong thing while `helper` -- a name with no meaning once the
; block closes -- silently rides along. See doc/kurt-soundness.md.
;
; note: this can't use `expect` (16-expect.kurt) -- the failure happens while *closing*
; the `let` block itself (triggered by dedenting below), at which point the current
; mode is still `let`, not `expect`; `expect` can only ever observe an error raised by
; an ordinary statement directly inside its own body, never a preceding block's own
; closing check. Hence the older `;;; ` marker convention here instead.
load logic
bool P
const z
use P z
let x
    const helper
    use P helper
    P helper
true
;;; ProofError: there are constant symbols
